messages.body:
Lead (revision 3): All three are taken, under the new numbering (§10):
- the thread unit (now 3) and the start-up (now 2) both need the kernel op
  (unit 1);
- the drivers' move (now 8) needs SO-137 merged as well as unit 2;
- the template's move (now 11) needs units 4 and 5.

Every unit now states what it needs.

Lead (revision 5): SO-142 changed after revision 3. Its revision now under review (revision 3,
`09a3cf99` in its worktree) holds:
- the per-thread register: `TPIDR_EL0` saved and loaded at each switch on
  arm64, starting zero;
- the lazily numbered lock owner in the register (`sos.rt`'s `rt_lock_owner`);
- a bad free as an ordinary `panic("bad free at {}")`;
- the `double_free` and `stray_free` cases at `EXIT_PANIC`, with no 66.

The design now cites these as "as SO-142's revision under review stands"
(§4.3, §4.7, §5.3), not as merged. §5.3 says that if SO-142 merges without
the per-thread register, unit 2 takes that kernel change and the hook with
it. §5.3 also sets who writes the register's content and when:
- the kernel writes zero at the thread's start;
- `_start`'s step 1 writes the main block's address;
- the trampoline's first act writes the start record's address;
- the lock-owner hook writes a number, made odd in unit 3, at a still-zero
  register's first lock;
- after that, nothing writes it again.
